 pardon's X marked a chamber sealed a century. Inside: not gold — at first. A tomb. A logbook. The real a royal pardon: the captain's confession, naming every cache along Smuggler's Cove.\n\nThe island rose from the mist like a fist. a royal pardon led them past the reef — the reef that had the bones of a dozen ships in its teeth — to a beach of black sand. 'Eats ships,' the gunner muttered. 'Charming.'"}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Reckoning', 'text': "Off the headland, Captain Jack Thorne opened the logbook one last time. So many caches. So many voyages. freedom has a price — the sea had tested it, and it held.\n\nthe gunner fought like three people. The crew fought like the sea itself. Captain Jack Thorne faced Captain Blackheart with cutlasses and old scores, and the duel was the kind they sing about: give, take, blood in the sand, neither yielding.\n\nThey sailed for Smuggler's Cove with the gold and a royal pardon and the logbook's secrets. The crew's shares would make them rich. Captain Jack Thorne's share would make them legend. the gunner was already spending theirs on a tavern."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'Port and Pardon', 'text': "Captain Jack Thorne took the logbook to the governor — not Captain Blackheart's governor, the new one — and traded certain pages for a pardon, signed and sealed. The crew cheered. Respectability, it turns out, is its own adventure.\n\nSmuggler's Cove welcomed them with bells and rum. The gold was divided fair — the articles are the articles. a royal pardon's copies went to every crew member who'd earned one. The original stayed with Captain Jack Thorne.\n\nthe gunner's tavern opened to packed crowds: 'The a royal pardon.' The sign showed Captain Jack Thorne with a cutlass and a grin. Captain Jack Thorne pretended to mind. Nobody believed it."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'The Tide Returns', 'text': "freedom has a price. Full sail. The horizon, as always, was just the beginning.\n\nCaptain Blackheart's successor was out there somewhere — there's always a successor. Captain Jack Thorne wasn't worried. The sea decides, and the sea loves the bold.\n\nThe new crew was young and wide-eyed. Captain Jack Thorne read them the articles, including the added one. They cheered, the way crews do. Pirates are sentimentalists. It's why they survive."}]}

